// Bail-out 4: a numeric key whose printed text is not the property it names.
//
// `1.0` prints as `1.0` but names the property `1`, so `{1.0: .., "1": ..}` is one property written
// twice. Ordering by printed text ranks the two spellings differently, which breaks the tie that
// preserves their relative order and silently flips which one wins -- the formatter changing what the
// document means. Refusing is the only safe answer, so the key reports no identity at all.
//
// The test is a round-trip (`String(Number(k)) == k`), not a shape: `/decimal`, `/exponent`, `/hex`
// and `/separator` are four spellings of the same hazard, and `numeric-keys.json` holds the keys that
// do round-trip and are read normally.
//
// The refusal survives its own output, which is what makes it correct: both key writers quote a
// numeric key only when it round-trips, so a key refused here is emitted bare and is still a
// non-round-tripping numeric literal on the next pass. Were it quoted, pass 2 would see an ordinary
// string key and reorder.
//
// `/ordered` has no numeric key and is reordered, so the refusal is per-object, not per-document.
{
  "openapi": "3.0.0",
  "paths": {
    "/decimal": { "get": { "responses": {}, 1.0: "first", "1": "last", "operationId": "op" } },
    "/exponent": { "get": { "responses": {}, 1e2: "first", "100": "last", "operationId": "op" } },
    "/hex": { "get": { "responses": {}, 0x10: "first", "16": "last", "operationId": "op" } },
    "/separator": { "get": { "responses": {}, 1_000: "first", "1000": "last", "operationId": "op" } },
    "/ordered": { "get": { "responses": {}, "summary": "s", "operationId": "op" } }
  }
}
